titre $ Key to the agaricoid genera of the complex
1,2 $ Basidiomata as in Tricholomopsis or Lentinula, fruiting directly on exposed wood, often with ephemeral or persistent partial veil; pileipellis an ixotrichoderm, ixohymenoderm or polycystoderm with mostly inflated and branched filamentous hyphae or clavate terminal elements but sometimes becoming cutis-like by maturity
1,8 $ Basidiomata as in Collybia or Clitocybe, probably arising from buried wood, without annulus (except H. velata), usually with distinct pseudorhiza; pileipellis a continuous and regular hymeniderm, dry or viscid, with or without thick- or thin-walled pileosetae
2,3 $ Basidiomata without persistent annulus (annulus may remain as fugacious remnants or a subtle ridge on stipe); pileus surface sometimes with integral patches resembling velar tissue
2,7 $ Basidiomata with persistent annulus (absent in Armillaria tabescens)
3,4 $ Basidiomata small (pileus rarely exceeding 30 mm broad); pileus and lamellae various
3,5 $ Basidiomata larger; pileus surface smooth, moist to viscid; lamellae white to cream-colored, thin
4,0 $ Basidiomata erect, not as Tricholomopsis; pileus surface granulose-ftoccose, bright yellow-gold to yellow-orange; lamellae thick, white, subdistant: pantropical $ Cyptotrama*
4,0 $ Basidiomata fruiting laterally, like Tricholomopsis: pileus surface brown, smooth; lamellae thin, white; neotropics $ Pselldohiatula*
5,0 $ Spores omamented, warty; pileus surface bright pink to coral-colored, wrinkled; lamellae thin, pallid pink; basidiomata fruiting laterally, like Tricholomopsis; temperate zone $ Rhodotus*
5,6 $ Spores smooth; pileus not pink or coral-colored
6,0 $ Pantropical, extending into South Temperate Zone; pileipellis an ixohymenoderm, ixotrichoderm or polycystoderm; pleuro- and cheilocystidia prominent; spores> 14   µm diam, subglobose, thick-walled. . . . . . . . . . .. $ Oudemansiella
6,0 $ Temperate; pileipellis an ixohymenoderm of branched termini or inflated cells with multiple slender outgrowths; spores of moderate size, usually ellipsoid, thin-walled; basidiomata medium-sized to large $ Flammulina*
7,0 $ Pileus viscid to glutinous; pileipellis an ixohymenoderm; pleurocystidia prominent; spores large, thick-walled, subglobose; temperate $ Mucidula
7,0 $ Pileus dry to moist, with minute pyramidal scales over the disc; pileipellis a cutis of repent hyphae; pleurocystidia few and inconspicuous; spores usually thin-walled, rarely subglobose; cosmopolitan $ Armillaria*
8,0 $ Spores omamented with awl-shaped spines; pileipellis viscid; neotropics; basidiomata often diminutive $ Dactvlosporillll
8,9 $ Spores smooth; pileipellis dry; cosmopolitan; basidiomata collybioid, moderate to large
9,0 $ Fruiting on dead strobili of conifers or Magnolia $ Strobilllrus*
9,10 $ Basidiomatal origin obscure, probably on buried wood but not strobili of conifers or Magnolia; pseudorhizal
10,0 $ Basidiomata marasmioid, gracile, radicating; pileus dry; stipe black, cartilaginous $ Rhizomarasmius*
10,11 $ Basidiomata stouter, collybioid; stipe not black
11,12 $ Pileus surface dry: pileipellis composed of inflated pileocystidia and thinto thick-walled setoid elements; North Temperate
11,13 $ Pileus surface moist to glutinous; pileipellis composed of inflated pileocystidia, with or without thin-walled piJeaJ hairs
12,0 $ PiJeosetae thick-walled (wall-7   µm thick), stiff, brittle, blond to rufous brown $ Xerula
12,0 $ Pileosetae thin-walled (wall -2   µm thick), flexuous, proximally inflated, hyaline to blond $ Paraxeru!a
13,0 $ Eastern and western Australia; pileus citrine- to olive-green: spores ellipsoid, thick-walled $ Protoxeru!a
13,0$* Cosmopolitan; pileus some shade of brown or olive-brown (sometimes pallid to almost white); spores subglobose, ellipsoid to limoniform, thinwalled$Hymenopellis
